    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Evidence that loss of CFTR drives dysbiosis**  \n- **Intestinal epithelial‑specific Cftr KO mice** develop marked shifts in microbial composition (↑Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae, Clostridia, mucolytic taxa) without changes in diet, confirming that CFTR loss in epitheli is sufficient to induce dysbiosis【PMID:37546931】(animal).  \n- **Whole‑body Cftr‑KO versus gut‑corrected CFTR‑KO mice** show dysbiosis only when intestinal CFTR is absent; restoring intestinal CFTR normalizes the microbiota, directly linking CFTR function to microbial community structure【PMID:38554847】(animal).  \n- **Cftr deletion in epithelial vs. immune cells** demonstrates that epithelial loss primarily alters large‑intestine microbiota, while immune‑cell loss affects the small intestine, indicating CFTR’s cell‑type‑specific role in shaping the gut microbiome【PMID:36289287】(animal).  \n- **Systematic review of human and animal studies** reports consistent reductions in diversity and enrichment of pro‑inflammatory taxa in CF patients, attributing these changes to CFTR dysfunction rather than solely to therapy or diet【PMID:36600119】(human/animal).\n\n**Evidence that dysbiosis promotes intestinal inflammation**  \n- **iCftr KO mice** exhibit elevated fecal calprotectin (a neutrophil‑derived inflammatory marker) concurrently with dysbiotic signatures, showing that the microbial shift is accompanied by measurable inflammation【PMID:37546931】(animal).  \n- **CFTR‑KO mice** display increased intestinal permeability, mucosal inflammation, and peri‑portal liver inflammation; treatment with non‑absorbable antibiotics reduces both dysbiosis and inflammation, indicating a causal contribution of the dysbiotic microbiota【PMID:38554847】(animal).  \n- **Ivacaftor treatment in CF patients** reduces Enterobacteriaceae abundance and correlates with a fall in stool calprotectin, linking improvement of dysbiosis to decreased intestinal inflammation in humans【PMID:30546102】(human).  \n- **Reviews** note a bidirectional relationship where dysbiosis and low‑grade inflammation reinforce each other in CF, supporting the mechanistic link【PMID:35160099】(human/animal).\n\n**Overall mechanistic path (CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation)**  \nThe animal models provide direct causative evidence that CFTR loss initiates dysbiotic alterations, and that these alterations are sufficient to elicit inflammatory responses. Human data corroborate the association and show that therapeutic restoration of CFTR function or modulation of the microbiota attenuates inflammation. Together, the literature supports a stepwise mechanistic chain from CFTR deficiency through dysbiosis to intestinal inflammation.  \n\n*Conclusion: Multiple animal and human studies substantiate each step of the CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation pathway.*\n\nGraph-derived candidate insights\n- PATH 9ed6292d75395474 — GRAPH-INCREMENTAL CANDIDATE; multi-hop inference; HOP 1: cftr causes cystic fibrosis [PMID: 36289287]; HOP 2: cystic fibrosis leads_to inflammation [PMID: 33201261].\n- PATH 57416f2f7b9449cc — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; multi-hop inference; HOP 1: cftr induces dysbiosis [PMID: 37546931]; HOP 2: dysbiosis associated_with inflammation [PMID: 36600119]."
